  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nTo start the workweek, shortwave energy ejecting out of the \nsouthwest will result in very minor height falls that act to \ndecrease our highs by a few degrees, putting them closer to the mid-\n70s. Moisture will be too meager for any meaningful precipitation \nchances, though the NBM puts a small pocket of 20% PoPs in eastern \nKansas Monday night. All in all, the impact of the shortwave will be \nminor, and we will be back to regularly scheduled highs in the upper \n70s by Wednesday.\n\nGlobal models essentially depict the low pressure system currently \nin the desert southwest lifting sharply northwest of Missouri over \nthe course of the week and impacting the southern Plains and Midwest \nvery little. After the shortwave moves out Wednesday, we return to a \nzonal/slightly ridged pattern until potentially late week as some \ndisorganized shortwave energy trails in the wake of the more \ndominant troughing that lifted to the north. Models begin to diverge \nquite a bit on this scenario at this lead time, and while this could \nbe the next most probable rain maker for the area over Halloweekend, \nthere is little certainty at this time. If it pans out, we could \nalso see a slight decrease in temperatures in addition to rain, but \nsuch a weak synoptic feature likely wouldn't lower highs by much. \nThe NBM currently buys in to the precipitation scenario, and has an \ninterquartile range for highs on Halloween between 67 and 75 for \nSpringfield (63 being the normal high). We will be watching this\nfeature closely as models are able to better resolve it with\nshorter lead time.",
